  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Hot, dry, and smoky conditions continue with the persistent \n   weather pattern through Saturday.\n\n-  Dry thunderstorm potential: Thursday afternoon through Friday\n   morning across southwest and west-central Montana.\n\n-  Elevated winds and fire weather: Saturday and Sunday region-\n   wide.\n\nDry southwesterly flow on the western periphery of the upper-level\nridge will maintain hot, dry, and smoky conditions across the\nNorthern Rockies through the next several days. Air Quality\nAdvisories have been updated to remain in effect for Lemhi county\nthrough at least Friday. Expect degraded air quality and\nvisibility to persist.\n\nDespite the relatively opaque appearance of the sky due to\nregional wildfire smoke, intense late-July sunshine has still \nbeen able to overcome most smoke-shading effects. Temperatures\nhave still been able to climb to within a degree or two of their\nclear-sky potential, keeping daytime temperatures several degrees \nwarmer than normal for this time of year during the afternoon and\nevening hours.\n\nIsolated dry thunderstorm chances continue in southwest Montana\neach afternoon. Attention to detail in the forecast indicates the\nprogression of several weak shortwave troughs embedded in this \nsouthwesterly flow ejecting into the region Thursday afternoon \nthrough Friday morning . Strong mid-level lapse rates and\nsufficient shear combined with a very dry sub-cloud layer will \nsupport dry thunderstorms capable of producing lightning and \nstrong erratic outflow winds even during the overnight and early \nmorning hours.\n\nA broad longwave trough off the Pacific Coast heads inland this \nweekend breaking down the stubborn ridge that we have been dealing\nwith for quite some time. Strong pre-frontal warming will drive \ntemperatures near the 100 degree mark on Saturday particularly for\nvalleys along and south of I-90. \n\nAs the cooler airmass begins to pass through Saturday into \nSunday, expect widespread westerly winds sustaining 10-25 mph \nwith gusts of 25 to 40 mph across the region. This wind/heat/ and \nlow RH combination will significantly elevate fire weather \nconcerns and create hazardous, choppy conditions on lakes and \nwaterways. This system will remain mostly dry with no significant\nor notable precipitation expected, but temperatures will drop\n10-20 degrees behind the front for Sunday and Monday bringing\ntemperatures back to near and just below normal for this time of\nyear.",
